OL,2,W/*%+,"-/.!/*%/*-'Q(,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
OL,3,GFEATURESCBBC One announcers make the
OL,4,Ctransition from formal to friendly and
OL,5,Cconversational
OL,6,
OL,7,FOver the past few years dozens of clips
OL,8,Fof continuity from the days before home
OL,9,Frecording was commonplace have gone
OL,10,Fonline.
OL,12,FThey provide fascinating evidence of
OL,13,Fthe gradual evolution of presentation
OL,14,Fon BBC One.
OL,16,FIt is easy to think only of the
OL,17,Flandmarks such as changes to the idents
OL,18,For the end of in-vision continuity.
OL,23,APub: 03-Feb-2024Qpppppppppppppppppppppp
OL,24,ANext Page BRewind CNat Radio FNat Sport
FL,200,120,200,300,0,100
PN,13902
SC,0002
CT,3,C
PS,c008
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 2   F139A1786222103
OL,1,W{$~sj5j5h7}j7}                 G2/9
OL,2,W/*%+,"-/.!/*%/*-'Q(,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
OL,3,GFEATURESCBBC One announcers make the
OL,4,Ctransition from formal to friendly and
OL,5,Cconversational
OL,6,
OL,7,FBut it seems something much more subtle
OL,8,Fhappened. Scripting and delivery
OL,9,Fgradually became more informative and
OL,10,Fconversational.
OL,12,FOne of the greatest finds for the
OL,13,Fhistorian is a recording of most of the
OL,14,Fevening's junctions from the first
OL,15,FMonday of BBC One in colour in November
OL,16,F1969.
OL,23,APub: 03-Feb-2024Qpppppppppppppppppppppp
OL,24,ANext Page BRewind CNat Radio FNat Sport
FL,200,120,200,300,0,100
PN,13903
SC,0003
CT,3,C
PS,c008
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 2   F139A1786222103
OL,1,W{$~sj5j5h7}j7}                 G3/9
OL,2,W/*%+,"-/.!/*%/*-'Q(,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
OL,3,GFEATURESCBBC One announcers make the
OL,4,Ctransition from formal to friendly and
OL,5,Cconversational
OL,6,
OL,7,FAt a superficial level it isn't that
OL,8,Fdifferent to any other evening's BBC
OL,9,FOne presentation before the early 90s `
OL,10,Fgenerally there's one trail per
OL,11,Fjunction then an "alternative viewing"
OL,12,Fpointer for BBC Two gives way to the
OL,13,Fglobe to introduce the programme.
OL,15,FJohn Glover remained a familiar voice
OL,16,Funtil his retirement in 1985.
OL,18,FHowever listen to the actual words
OL,19,Fspoken. It is terse, to the point and
OL,20,Fvery formal: "On BBC Two now...on BBC
OL,21,FOne..."
OL,23,APub: 03-Feb-2024Qpppppppppppppppppppppp
OL,24,ANext Page BRewind CNat Radio FNat Sport
FL,200,120,200,300,0,100
PN,13904
SC,0004
CT,3,C
PS,c008
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 2   F139A1786222103
OL,1,W{$~sj5j5h7}j7}                 G4/9
OL,2,W/*%+,"-/.!/*%/*-'Q(,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
OL,3,GFEATURESCBBC One announcers make the
OL,4,Ctransition from formal to friendly and
OL,5,Cconversational
OL,6,
OL,7,FIt also looks just a little clumsy at
OL,8,Ftimes ` for example the drama Take
OL,9,FThree Girls is introduced over a
OL,10,Fpromotion slide then the globe appears
OL,11,Ffor a simple channel identification.
OL,13,FNow imagine the scripting a few years
OL,14,Flater:
OL,23,APub: 03-Feb-2024Qpppppppppppppppppppppp
OL,24,ANext Page BRewind CNat Radio FNat Sport
FL,200,120,200,300,0,100
PN,13905
SC,0005
CT,3,C
PS,c008
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 2   F139A1786222103
OL,1,W{$~sj5j5h7}j7}                 G5/9
OL,2,W/*%+,"-/.!/*%/*-'Q(,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
OL,3,GFEATURESCBBC One announcers make the
OL,4,Ctransition from formal to friendly and
OL,5,Cconversational
OL,6,
OL,7,F"Over on BBC Two now, the latest from
OL,8,Fthe world of medicine in Television
OL,9,FDoctor. Here on One, there's a major
OL,10,Fnew drama in 15 minutes. Liza Goddard
OL,11,Fleads the cast in the story of three
OL,12,Fyoung women living in London, Take
OL,13,FThree Girls. First at ten to nine the
OL,14,Fmain news with Kenneth Kendall."
OL,16,FI'm not claiming that script would have
OL,17,Fgot past the Presentation Editor
OL,18,Funpolished but you can see what I tried
OL,19,Fto do ` write an informative script
OL,20,Fwhich linked the programmes together in
OL,21,Fa friendly, conversational way.
OL,23,APub: 03-Feb-2024Qpppppppppppppppppppppp
OL,24,ANext Page BRewind CNat Radio FNat Sport
FL,200,120,200,300,0,100
PN,13906
SC,0006
CT,3,C
PS,c008
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 2   F139A1786222103
OL,1,W{$~sj5j5h7}j7}                 G6/9
OL,2,W/*%+,"-/.!/*%/*-'Q(,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
OL,3,GFEATURESCBBC One announcers make the
OL,4,Ctransition from formal to friendly and
OL,5,Cconversational
OL,6,
OL,7,FThere are other interesting examples on
OL,8,FYouTube. On an evening in 1971, BBC One
OL,9,Fgoes from Tomorrow's World to The
OL,10,FVirginian via a trail for Dixon of Dock
OL,11,FGreen.
OL,13,FBut nothing in the announcer's script
OL,14,Fis designed to hold the viewer. There's
OL,15,Fa plug for a Tomorrow's World book on
OL,16,Fone side of the junction and a straight
OL,17,Fchannel ID at the other.
OL,19,FA network closedown from December 1974
OL,20,Fis similarly to the point. There's no
OL,21,Ffriendly chat from the announcer ` just
OL,22,Fa timecheck then it's goodnight.
OL,23,APub: 03-Feb-2024Qpppppppppppppppppppppp
OL,24,ANext Page BRewind CNat Radio FNat Sport
FL,200,120,200,300,0,100
PN,13907
SC,0007
CT,3,C
PS,c008
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 2   F139A1786222103
OL,1,W{$~sj5j5h7}j7}                 G7/9
OL,2,W/*%+,"-/.!/*%/*-'Q(,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
OL,3,GFEATURESCBBC One announcers make the
OL,4,Ctransition from formal to friendly and
OL,5,Cconversational
OL,6,
OL,7,FI suspect there was never a point where
OL,8,Fscripting changed suddenly. Rather it
OL,9,Fwas an evolutionary process, no doubt
OL,10,Fpushed along by new personnel gradually
OL,11,Fcoming to the fore.
OL,13,FIn the early 70s, many of the people
OL,14,Fwho became legends of BBC Presentation
OL,15,F` David Allan, Andy Cartledge, Richard
OL,16,FStraker ` were usually on BBC Two.
OL,23,APub: 03-Feb-2024Qpppppppppppppppppppppp
OL,24,ANext Page BRewind CNat Radio FNat Sport
FL,200,120,200,300,0,100
PN,13908
SC,0008
CT,3,C
PS,c008
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 2   F139A1786222103
OL,1,W{$~sj5j5h7}j7}                 G8/9
OL,2,W/*%+,"-/.!/*%/*-'Q(,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
OL,3,GFEATURESCBBC One announcers make the
OL,4,Ctransition from formal to friendly and
OL,5,Cconversational
OL,6,
OL,7,FThere they were described as
OL,8,F"narrators" rather than announcers and
OL,9,Fa greater degree of informality was
OL,10,Fallowed ` both during the evening
OL,11,Fschedule and the trade test colour
OL,12,Ffilms.
OL,14,FDid some of that informality gradually
OL,15,Fspread to the main channel by stealth?
OL,16,FOr was it a conscious creative
OL,17,Fdecision?
OL,19,FIt certainly is worth noting that what
OL,20,Fmany of us would regard as the golden
OL,21,Fage of BBC Presentation was not created
OL,22,Fovernight.
OL,23,APub: 03-Feb-2024Qpppppppppppppppppppppp
OL,24,ANext Page BRewind CNat Radio FNat Sport
FL,200,120,200,300,0,100
